WEBVTT

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:08.950
This is the fifth in a series of quarterly reports that feature progress being made by NASA's Exploration Launch Projects Office,

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:14.810
which is developing the Ares I crew launch vehicle and designing the Ares V cargo launch vehicle.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:21.080
Last quarter, Ares reported on a new material for first-stage solid rocket motor insulation,

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:27.680
computer simulation of the vehicle's ascent environment, and injector tests conducted on J2X engine hardware.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.960
Work continued from last quarter.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.080
on the facility that will house Marshall's world-class friction stir weld tool.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.100
Testing also continued this quarter on the upper stage confidence panels

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.480
and Ares 1-X roll control system.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:45.930
This quarter, the Ares 1 design underwent significant wind tunnel testing

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:51.650
at both Langley Research Center's Unitary Wind Tunnel and Boeing's Polysonic Wind Tunnel.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:58.900
At Langley, tests focus on high-speed flow, simulating speeds from Mach 1.5 up to Mach 4.5.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:00:59.870
Boeing

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.860
testing tests were run at lower speeds from Mach 0.5 to Mach 1.6. Both wind tunnels also

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.480
tested a proposed new launch abort system configuration that incorporates an aerodynamic

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:17.180
fairing to reduce drag and aeroacoustic noise created by protuberances on the vehicle. As

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.880
part of the project's ongoing outreach and education efforts, Ares personnel attended

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:28.200
the finals of the Team America Rocketry Challenge. At the event, the project unveiled an Ares

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.960
one replica rocket, a

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.220
1 to 100 scale model of the new crew launch vehicle.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.440
Through participation in this event,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.900
the Exploration Launch Projects Office

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.980
demonstrates its commitment to inspiring

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.320
the next generation of rocket scientists and space advocates.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.060
At ATK's test facility, a solid rocket motor test firing

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.540
was conducted in partnership with the Space Shuttle Program

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.000
to qualify the new insulation material

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.420
and low temperature O-rings.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.540
The test also examined movement of the center segment

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.080
of the motor for structural sag.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.500
These data will help validate the model for the Ares 5-segment motor.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.870
Following this test, the T97 facility will be modified to accommodate the needs of the

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:09.810
Ares project.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:15.080
Risk reduction tests are underway on the first stage, Ascent Thrust Vector Controller.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:20.180
This controller will translate commands from the flight computer to the nozzle actuators

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.780
on the first stage, steering the vehicle during ascent.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.640
The testing has been a great success, identifying some modifications necessary to the design.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.960
Confidence panel testing continues.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.940
continues for the Ares I upper stage instrument unit.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.620
These tests are looking to increase the aspect ratio

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.120
of the instrument unit's height,

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.720
compared to the structural ribs

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.220
to reduce the component's weight.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.820
Further progress was also made this quarter

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.360
on the installation of Marshall's

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.400
robotic friction stir weld tool.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.920
The 70,000 pound main column of the robotic tool

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.020
was delivered to building 4755,

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.060
where facilities work had been completed last quarter

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.840
to receive the one of a kind machine.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.900
Once completed, this tool will be used to assemble

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.560
number of different upper stage components in the same manner as was developed under the external

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.710
tank program on the space shuttle. The Ares-1 interstage mock-up completed assembly this quarter

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:15.810
at Marshall. The mock-up provides full-scale representations of the flight design, providing

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:21.700
an accurate representation of the planned flight hardware. This simulator provides a cost-effective

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:26.920
means of visualizing and validating the vehicle design, as well as providing insight into the

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.740
challenges and solutions for future ground support activities.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.920
Work began this quarter on Heritage Saturn IV-B main propulsion system valves.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:43.770
The team, working from the sole remaining original drawing, have disassembled the locks and LH-2 vent relief valves.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:49.170
The individual components will be thoroughly cleaned and then reassembled for testing later this summer.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:55.390
In addition to advancing work on component development, this provides a valuable training tool for new engineers.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.970
Testing is underway on the Apollo Heritage J-2 liquid oxygen.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.960
oxygen inducer to verify the component's performance. Scaled water testing can be performed for a

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:10.480
fraction of the cost of full-scale testing, and tests can be conducted earlier in the development

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.880
phase of the project. To better understand the side loads placed on the engine nozzle, airflow testing

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.830
is underway at Marshall's Nozzle Test Facility. The results of these tests will help engineers

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:26.990
optimize the design of the engine nozzle, trimming the engine's weight to allow for larger payloads.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.980
Test today to have used a scale space shuttle.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.280
main engine nozzle, but upcoming tests will transition to a 1-50 scale J2X nozzle.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.500
A second round of tests was conducted on the Peacekeeper engine to evaluate its fitness

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.080
for use as an Aries 1-X roll control system.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:50.170
The decommissioned Peacekeeper hardware will get a new lease on life, saving taxpayers

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.350
significant expense.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.180
The results of this test, combined with those from the previous series reported last quarter,

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:59.820
confirm that the Peacekeeper engine can be reconfigured.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.080
application on ARIES 1-X. Testing began in early June to ensure the suitability of commercially

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:12.120
obtainable sensors for use on this first test flight in 2009. All the sensors passed in the

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:17.260
first set of tests completed in mid-June. Join us for the next ARIES quarterly report,

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.760
which will feature a wind tunnel testing update, the first stage parachute demonstration and

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.610
the latest developments on the upper stage and the J2X upper stage engine. For more information

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.130
about Aries Plans and Progress,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:29.970
visit us on the...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.860
web at nasa.gov slash ARIES.
